A||||13||Raydar||87-88 Duke - belt tensioner question||||8||
Z||000000||Raydar||08-09-2001||08:57 PM||||I'm in the process of taking my 88 coupe out of mothballs. Haven't driven it much in the past year. <BR>Ever since I've owned it, the tensioner pulley has sat at a bit of an angle. <BR>Bought Rodney's tensioner pulley replacement bearing (cheap gamble for $8), because I heard that's what usually wears out. <BR>Well... NOT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>The entire arm appears to be loose. Can this be fixed? I've heard that it's "not field repairable" but I'm looking for a second opinion. Is it in danger of just falling apart? Can I drive it this way for a few hundred miles? I'd hate to buy a new tensioner and then have to mess with changing it for an engine that I'm going to remove, shortly. <BR>Any help is greatly appreciated.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-09-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||dman||08-09-2001||09:46 PM||||Hi Raydar. A new tensioner is gonna cost about $50 to $60 bucks from your GM dealer. Its not difficult to replace (hmmm, an hour or so at most). If you can afford to eat that cost then I'd recommend doing it rather than getting stranded somewhere should that serpentine belt slip off and fail.<P>But then again, you say its been like that for a while so the risk isn't really any greater than it was before.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||08-09-2001||10:02 PM||||What a timely topic!!<P>For quite awhile there has been a squeal coming from my engine.  Normally just when you first start the car up and then it goes away. Quite awhile ago I replaced the water pump and put on a new belt and it didn't do a thing.  Figured it must be the alternator bearings going out.  Last night when my son was driving home he said the ligts started dimming and the car died just as he pulled into the driveway.  I figure the alternator has finally gone.<P>When I went out to pull the belt off I found it was just about shredded.  While looking at the tensioner, I put a wrench on it to see wheich way I was supposed to turn it to give me the slack I need when I eventually put a belt back on.  I could not turn it to the right (facing the tensioner) and when I turned to the left the bolt that holds the pulley on came loose and I could remove the pulley.  With the pulley in place, I can slide it toward the front of the car and to the back.  Isn't there supposed to be some spring tension here somewhere??  I'm really not too sure what I am looking at or for here.  I just read Ogre's article on it but am still not sure how this thing works or if mine is just gone...<P>Any info would be most appreciated!!<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-09-2001||11:03 PM||||read in me cave..... The tensioner arm cannot be repaired. it must be replaced. If the belt is not running straight on all pulleys then it is running with uneven tension across the belt width and will not last. Add this to heat issues and it could go at any minute... there is no way to know how long it will run like that. If you are going to drive like this, cary a belt (the non AC one is easier to install from the top) and a wrench. <P>With a 15mm wrench on the pully bolt, rotate CCW (toward battery) to unload the belt. If it won't move, the arm is bad and must be replaced.<P>before installing a new belt. carefully check the pulleys. all of them. make sure nothing is packed into the grooves or built up on the peaks. Clean all pulleys with Simple Green, CitriSolve or something similar even if you use solvents to remove heavy dirt. Any petrolium residue will kill a belt quick.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||08-09-2001||11:55 PM||||Thanks for your replies, guys.<BR>I suppose I will replace it. Aside from having the pulley cocked at a strange angle, it doesn't seem to have as much travel or tension as I would expect. <BR>And no, the belt is <I>not</I> fun to put on from the top, even with the battery out. It's like trying to giftwrap a pile of Jello. In the dark. <BR>Good vocabulary exercise.<P>Mbramble, sounds like yours is even more toasted than mine.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-09-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||mbramble||08-10-2001||07:28 AM||||Thanks for the info.  Can anyone tell me if this is the part I need --  <A HREF="http://www.carparts.com/parts/sample.asp?reflink=42984-0-0-0-0&SegmentId=1&parttypeid=19018&vehicleid=42984&catalogid=&partnum=NONE&dc=" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carparts.com/parts/sample.asp?reflink=42984-0-0-0-0&SegmentId=1&parttypeid=19018&vehicleid=42984&catalogid=&partnum=NONE&dc=</A>   -- the first item on the page "Component: Belt Tensioner.  Is that the whole unit that I need to replace??<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||08-10-2001||08:59 AM||||Your link didn't work, but I think I found where you were. <P>Check these links... <A HREF="http://www.gates.com/shared/brochure.cfm?brochure=121&site_section=automotive" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gates.com/shared/brochure.cfm?brochure=121&site_section=automotive</A> <BR>and <A HREF="http://www.gates.com/automotive/sublevel.cfm?&site_subsection=Tensioners%2FPulley" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gates.com/automotive/sublevel.cfm?&site_subsection=Tensioners%2FPulley</A> <P>I'm still not clear as to whether your link is just the pulley, or the entire assembly. It's way cheaper than I expected.<BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||08-10-2001||01:40 PM||||Yea, I can't tell if it's the whole unit or just the pulley.  Called the customer support number for carparts.com and it was answered by the JC Whitney customer folks! The girl said they were completely lost as to what is available at carparts.com until they get a bit more organized after the merger or acquizition or whatever it was.  I sent an e-mail to carparts.com asking them what that particular it is -- the pulley or the whole idler assembly.  Hope to hear something from them...||||reg||1
Z||000008||mbramble||08-10-2001||11:22 PM||||So, what other cars can I look at to get a 'new' tensioner at the yard??  I plan on going in the morning to see if I can find one.  I checked all around town this afternoon and all that I was able to find was the pulley itself.  The Pontiac dealer parts dept was closed by the time I called them.  I would like to find one at the yard and then maybe order a new one from Pontiac.  It was not hard at all to pull off so changing it isn't too big a deal.  Now getting the alternator out, that was fun!!  But, I got a new one, and a belt tonight and will go ahead and put the alternator in tomorrow and then I just need the tensioner.<P>I can see the big spring inside.  Looks like a part of the face of the tensioner that holds one end of the sping in place broke off.  So, when you turn the thing, the spring can't catch and hold tension.  <p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 08-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||08-11-2001||02:11 PM||||Because of the alternator brakcet, the tensioner is Fiero specific. You can't get it off another car. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||mbramble||08-11-2001||09:53 PM||||So I found out today as I was looking around the yards!!  I did find one Fiero with the tensioner assembly still in it but the arm that the alternator mounts to was broken off.  So, I took the spring assembly off that one and installed it in mine!!  Yes, I know they can't be repaired but I did it and it works great.  It was quite a bit of fun because you have to load up that heavy spring to get it put together right because of the notches inside that limit the travel of the tensioner assembly.  I screwed the tensioner assembly/frame? down to my wooden workbench so that it wouldn't move.  Then I put the outer plate on with the spring in place and then started the bolt that holds it together.  I bolted a box end wrench over the boss that the pulley mounts to so that I could manuever it without the wrench popping off due to all the tension applied.  After using the wrench to swing the plate into proper position, I finished tightening the center bolt.  Let go and everything held and worked properly.  Installed it in the car and it lines up perfectly straight and works great.  Only problem I had was I must have not gotten the form-a-gasket on thick or even enough and it leaked and I had to pull it and remount it.  Installed the new alternator and belt and we are back on the road.<P>OK, so what is the proper way of getting the serpentine belt on??  The tensioner never seems to go far enough for a new belt and I have to work like crazy to get it over the lip of the pulley.  What's the best method??  That's the 3rd belt I've put on the car and it is a pain everytime.  Not quite as bad today because I'm working out a method that seems to work ok.  Actually I had to put it on twice today because of the leak from the tensioner assembly.  Just wondering about the correct way.<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||08-12-2001||12:46 AM||||I'm not sure I've done it the same way twice.... I think I usually put the idler under the belt last. <P>I wish you would have told us what you were going to try.... I would have told you to lube the thing with silicone/synthetic brake grease. It takes the heat better than allot of regular grease. That or maybe wheel bearing grease. Anything made for lots of heat. Part of what kills them is that the old grease is not up to the job.<P>The big reason I say don't mess with the arm is most people would not take the care you did in trying to do it safely.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mbramble||08-12-2001||01:17 PM||||I did put a thin coat of wheel bearing grease around the inside though I'm not really sure why it is needed.  Inside the unit there is just a very thick coiled spring(3 coils if I remember right).  The front plate 'floats' on the spring (and inside a plastic ring that surrounds the outside of it) and a little plastic piece wedges against the inside of the front plate and the end of the spring so that the spring doesn't slip and it can be loaded.  That's just where mine was broken, where the end of the spring sits on the back of the plate and the plastic wedge was gone.  The only possible movement inside is that the plate is allowed to rotate about an inch between two stops cast into the frame.  A little finger cast on the back of the plate catches on one of the two stops as the plate travels back and forth to tighten/loosen the tension on the spring.  <P>The 'donor' front plate had a metal bushing in the shaft where the hex bolt holds it to the frame.  The one off my car had a plastic bushing insert instead.  I drove out the metal bushing from the donor and replaced it with my plastic one.  The metal bushing was wallowed out on one side so it was worn pretty badly.  That seems like an improvement from the one to the other.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||08-12-2001||01:45 PM||||I was mainly concerned with lubing the pivot bolt and seal surface... I imagine the plastic bushing is self lubing. ||||reg||1
